#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068470 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ff9486fe-3ee1-5917-88de-97251c889552" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Function is Left- or Right-Continuous From a Graph"}] :question/id #uuid "0acc185c-cc26-523f-981a-3e024f0693a6" :question/math-academy-id "e-3589" :question/problem "Is the function $y=g(x)$ below left-continuous at $x=0$?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/474/Source/Images/365a2e8f5b5d5e0b8872647cb38cea17.png)" :question/worked-solution "To determine whether $g(x)$, is left-continuous at $x=0$, we need to check whether the left-sided limit is equal to the function value.\n\nIndeed, we see that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 0^-} g(x) = -2 = g(0)\n$$\n\nTherefore, $g(x)$ is left-continuous at $x=0$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068471 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2dc967c7-840e-5717-98f2-330fcf0fe12d" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Definition of Left- and Right-Continuity to Compute a Limit"}] :question/id #uuid "e9a8cbce-3bed-567b-9e0e-e1382465a681" :question/math-academy-id "e-3590" :question/problem "If $f(x)$ is right-continuous at $x=3$ and $f(3)=1$, then what is $\\displaystyle{\\lim_{x\\to 3^+}} \\left(f(x)-x^2\\right)$?" :question/worked-solution "Since $f(x)$ is right-continuous at $x=3$, we must have\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 3^+} f(x)=f(3)=1\n$$\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\nlim_(x → 3^{+})(f(x) - x^{2}) = 1 - 3^{2} =-8\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068472 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "81247553-750f-5bf7-a1c4-698ad63b5ae4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding Left- and Right-Continuity"}] :question/id #uuid "1ec6e675-6052-5cec-8b29-766084245f74" :question/math-academy-id "e-3591" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true in relation to the function $y=g(x)$ above?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/474/Source/Images/412190f4780d9243ce6ed015c2f67bf6.png)\n\n1. The function is left-continuous at $x=0$\n2. The function is right-continuous at $x=0$\n3. The function is continuous at $x=0$" :question/worked-solution "Let's look at each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is false. The function is not left-continuous at $x=0$, since the left-sided limit is not equal to the function value:\n$\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to0^-}g(x) = 2 \\neq -2 = g(0)$\n- Statement II is true. The function is right-continuous at $x=0$, since the right-sided limit is equal to the function value:\n$\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to0^+}g(x) = -2 = g(0)$\n- Statement III is false. In order for the function to be continuous at $x=0$, it must be both left-continuous and right-continuous. However, we have already determined that the function is not left-continuous at this point.\n\nIn conclusion, only statement II is true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069233 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2dc967c7-840e-5717-98f2-330fcf0fe12d" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Definition of Left- and Right-Continuity to Compute a Limit"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "95989bbf-ce3e-5609-a6a5-2c014e7cdf61" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066930} {:answer/id #uuid "9838f976-965f-5ac3-88b2-f5c36bcbb4bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066931} {:answer/id #uuid "c9e2bdb5-28c1-5e47-9d71-63e88a5fa7f5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066932} {:answer/id #uuid "594c783e-3765-5127-90ab-1555565cfd08" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066933} {:answer/id #uuid "61e2cd6e-d72f-5c5d-807c-39fcd802585c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066934}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "594c783e-3765-5127-90ab-1555565cfd08" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066933} :answer-field/id #uuid "9d02f6db-f390-53f2-926e-7229d57f1fd4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066929}] :question/id #uuid "b82792e4-e045-5093-b138-2f1c0f9db676" :question/math-academy-id "q-38328" :question/problem "If $f(x)$ is right-continuous at $x = 2$ and $lim_(x → 2^{+})f(x) =-1$, then what is $lim_(x → 2^{+})(f(x) + f(2))$?"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069235 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "81247553-750f-5bf7-a1c4-698ad63b5ae4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding Left- and Right-Continuity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10ba3cee-beb5-5de3-b100-b4659399a29b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066942} {:answer/id #uuid "3470f400-ebd9-5d32-b18a-976db2abfe55" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066943} {:answer/id #uuid "35964aec-3f92-51fb-aa69-3ca00fc71286" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066944} {:answer/id #uuid "439442ad-0a59-5a25-8fdf-5fc9f989cad7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066945} {:answer/id #uuid "7d5cc722-d385-5744-acb7-7fa6ba2176de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066946}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "10ba3cee-beb5-5de3-b100-b4659399a29b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066942} :answer-field/id #uuid "b91092ed-69cb-59c8-ab12-a23d55bdda8e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066941}] :question/id #uuid "ff6510c0-b13d-5580-9ffe-72088a4d30a3" :question/math-academy-id "q-38548" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/474/Source/Images/q-38548.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true in relation to the function $y = f(x)$ above?\n\n1. The function is left-continuous at $x =-1$\n2. The function is right-continuous at $x =-1$\n3. The function is continuous at $x =-1$"}]}
